# Copy to .env.local and fill in. NEVER commit the filled copy -- .gitignore
# excludes .env*, and this example is the one deliberate exception.
#
# None of this is needed to read the dashboard. Every run page, score and arc
# renders from the artifacts committed under reports/runs/, so a clone can
# `npm install && npm run build` and see everything with no key at all.
#
# A key is needed for ONE feature: reading a supplement label from a photo
# (POST /api/analyze-label). Without it that route returns
# `analyzer_unavailable` (503) and the rest of the site is unaffected.

# --- the model boundary (lib/analyze/llm.ts) ---------------------------------
#
# ONE key powers every model call the app makes: the label vision read, the
# ingredient-compatibility fill-in and the company profile (POST /api/scan), and
# the evidence-only label read (POST /api/analyze-label). Provider is CONFIG:
# any OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint works. Defaults are DeepSeek
# (founder 2026-09-07).

# Preferred key variable. VISION_API_KEY and GEMINI_API_KEY are still read as
# fallbacks because earlier deployments set them (the Vercel GEMINI_API_KEY
# actually holds a DeepSeek key, see HANDOFF.md).
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
VISION_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Endpoint. Default https://api.deepseek.com/chat/completions. VISION_API_URL
# is accepted as the legacy name. For the Gemini free tier:
#   MODEL_API_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/chat/completions
MODEL_API_URL=
VISION_API_URL=

# Model ids. Verify against the provider before setting -- an id copied from a
# pricing table that the API does not accept fails every call (the grok-4.3
# lesson). Defaults: deepseek-flash (vision), deepseek-chat (text). The old
# deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp id is retired; DeepSeek serves it with deepseek-flash.
LABEL_MODEL=
TEXT_MODEL=

# --- kill switch ------------------------------------------------------------

# Set to 0 to disable label reading even when a key is present. Anything else
# (or unset) leaves it enabled. Used to force the demo to answer only from
# full-pipeline scored runs.
LABEL_ANALYZER_ENABLED=

# --- waitlist (email capture from the QR roll-up) ---------------------------

# Supabase project URL, e.g. https://abcdefgh.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=

# The SERVICE ROLE key, NOT the anon key. It bypasses row level security, so it
# must stay server-side: note there is no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, which is what
# keeps Next.js from inlining it into a client bundle. The waitlist table has
# RLS on with no policies, so the anon key cannot reach it at all -- that is
# deliberate, and it is why the browser never talks to Supabase directly.
#
# Run docs/waitlist.sql once in the Supabase SQL editor to create the table.
# Without both variables, POST /api/waitlist returns waitlist_unavailable (503)
# and the rest of the site is unaffected.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- scan-run history (lib/scan-history/store.ts) --------------------------
#
# Reuses the SAME SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above -- one
# project, two tables (docs/waitlist.sql, docs/scan-history.sql). Every
# accepted POST /api/scan run (photo or manual) is recorded: the complete
# returned JSON, the request facts, the terminal status, this exact app
# release, and, for a photo, the original image in the private `scan-images`
# storage bucket (bucket/path/MIME/bytes/SHA-256 only -- never base64 in the
# database). Without both variables, a scan still answers normally and its
# `persistence.status` reports "unavailable".
#
# Set to "1" to make persistence REQUIRED: a run whose history could not be
# durably recorded is answered with a failure instead of the analysis, rather
# than serving an unrecorded result. Leave unset for local/test builds.
SCAN_HISTORY_REQUIRED=

# --- sign-in / email capture (lib/auth/supabase-browser.ts, POST /api/scan/claim) ---
#
# Google sign-in while a scan's results load (2026-09-16, founder: "people log
# in once so we capture their email"). Set ALL THREE to turn this on; leave
# any one unset and the deployment behaves exactly as before -- no sign-in
# card, no locked results, no script loaded from accounts.google.com. These
# ARE meant for the browser (NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, unlike every other var in
# this file) -- the anon key is subject to Postgres row level security, and
# the Google client id is not a secret.
#
# Setup (see docs/SYSTEM_DESIGN.md §7 for the full walkthrough):
#   1. Supabase dashboard -> Authentication -> Providers -> Google: enable it,
#      paste the Google OAuth Web client ID and secret from Google Cloud
#      Console.
#   2. Google Cloud Console -> that OAuth client -> Authorized JavaScript
#      origins: add https://bs-proof-dashboard.vercel.app and
#      http://localhost:3000.
#   3. The SAME client ID goes in NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID below.
#
# Reuses the SAME Supabase project as SUPABASE_URL above, but the anon key is
# a DIFFERENT credential from SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY -- never put the
# service role key here, it would be shipped to every visitor's browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# --- authenticated scans and private history (2026-10-03) --------------------
#
# SERVER-VERIFIED identity. Every server-side check asks Supabase Auth
# (GET {SUPABASE_URL}/auth/v1/user) who a bearer token belongs to; a token is
# never decoded and trusted locally, and a user id / email supplied by the
# client (body, form or header) is never read. "Signed in with Google" is read
# from Supabase's own `identities` / `app_metadata`, never from `user_metadata`
# (which the user can edit). This needs the server-only SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above -- NOT the NEXT_PUBLIC_ ones.
#
# SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH: when on, BOTH model-spending POST routes -- POST /api/scan
# (the /scan page) AND POST /api/analyze-label (the /tester page) -- refuse
# (BEFORE the body is read and before any model call) a request that does not
# carry a valid Google-backed Supabase bearer token: 401 {status:"unauthorized"}
# for a missing, invalid, expired or non-Google token, 503
# {status:"auth_unavailable"} when this deployment has no SUPABASE_URL /
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY or Supabase Auth cannot answer. Real scan results
# then depend on a Google sign-in -- nobody can scan anonymously through either
# route. (GET /api/analyze-label only reports capabilities, calls no model and
# stays public, like the static retained-run pages.)
#
# WHAT THIS IS, HONESTLY: identity, not a budget. ANY Google account that can
# sign in to this Supabase project can scan; there is no per-user quota, rate
# limit or allow-list (the operator wants open Google login). Whoever holds a
# Google account can still spend model calls, so it narrows who spends them and
# lets the owner see who -- it does not cap how much. Whether the shared
# project accepts open Google sign-ups is a shared setting this app does not
# change and has not verified.
#
# Fail-closed parsing: unset, empty, 0, false, no and off leave it OFF; ANY
# other value (1, true, even a typo) turns it ON.
#
# LOCAL / CI DEFAULT: leave it UNSET. Anonymous POST /api/scan then works
# exactly as it did before (no credentials, no Supabase, existing tests and the
# demo keep passing) and records NO owner. Even then, a bearer token that IS
# sent is verified and binds ownership, and an invalid one is a 401 -- it never
# silently downgrades to anonymous. Do NOT leave it unset in production.
#
# To run a production deployment on real, private, Google-gated scans, IN THIS
# ORDER (docs/SYSTEM_DESIGN.md section 7d is the full runbook):
#   1. Do the PREFLIGHT at the top of docs/scan-history.sql (the Supabase project
#      is SHARED with other apps: confirm no scan_* table, no scan-images bucket
#      and no unscoped storage.objects policy exists), then apply that file once
#      in the Supabase SQL Editor (creates scan_runs, scan_users and the PRIVATE
#      scan-images bucket; RLS on, no policies, anon/authenticated revoked).
#      SQL FIRST: with the variables below set and the tables missing, every
#      scan spends its model call and then fails to record.
#   2. Only then set SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH=1, SCAN_HISTORY_REQUIRED=1 (exactly 1 --
#      any other spelling leaves durability OFF, unlike SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH),
#      SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY and the three NEXT_PUBLIC_
#      sign-in variables above, and redeploy (the public ones are inlined at
#      build).
#   3. SUPABASE_URL must be EXACTLY the same project URL as
#      NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY that project's
#      service-role key (not the anon key). A mismatch is reported as a 401 --
#      Supabase rejects the key -- so it looks like an expired session and signs
#      people out; check these two values first when everyone is bounced.
#
# RETENTION: with SCAN_HISTORY_REQUIRED=1 every scan -- the full result, the
# signed-in person's Google email and, for a photo scan, the original photo --
# is kept INDEFINITELY. There is no expiry job, no deletion endpoint and no
# "delete my data" control; removal today is the project owner deleting rows and
# storage objects by hand. The app shows no retention notice yet. Deciding the
# notice and building deletion are open owner decisions: do not promise users
# deletion until it exists.
#
# GET /api/scan/history and GET /api/scan/history/[id] ALWAYS require a valid
# Google-backed token, whatever SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH says: they return only the
# caller's own 20 most recent runs (no photos, no image paths, no emails), and a
# run that is not theirs, has no owner or does not exist is the same 404.
# POST /api/scan/claim only CONFIRMS a run already belongs to the caller; it
# can never assign or transfer ownership.
SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH=

# --- front door ------------------------------------------------------------

# (nothing to configure)
#
# The public page at / is the waitlist and ONLY the waitlist. The label scanner
# lives at /tester, which is a separate route rather than a flag on the
# homepage: a switch that can put the scanner in front of the public is a
# switch that eventually does, by accident.
#
# /tester is UNLISTED, not protected -- no nav link, and the site is
# robots: index false -- so it stays out of search and out of a visitor's way,
# but the PAGE does not stop anyone who knows the URL, and the retained runs on
# it stay open. The label scanner on it is gated: with SCAN_REQUIRE_AUTH on it
# needs the same Google sign-in as /scan (POST /api/analyze-label, above). That
# is identity, not "only these people": if it ever needs an allow-list or a
# quota, that is a separate change.
